{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1531268-ad-d-to-ascension-spell-conversions","id":1531268,"title":"AD&D to Ascension Spell Conversions:","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-02-21T20:12:13-08:00","id":1531268,"title":"AD&D to Ascension Spell Conversions:","content":"Today I was thinking on what would be required in M:tA, to recreate the spells out of AD&amp;D (2e). Now, back in the day, when I played D&amp;D, I disliked mages and magic in my games (but that changed after I read <i>Ars Magica</i>). <br /> <br /> To recreate the 3rd-level spell, Monster Summoning I, you create 2d4 first-level monsters out of thin air, HOLY SMOKES BATMAN! So do that in Mage, you'd need Prime 3, and Life or Matter or both, at level,, Five! Yikes! As they only exist for a few rounds, they don't need a mind, so Life 5 works just fine for them, but, yeah. Vulgar-magic. <br /> <br /> I thought Monster-Summoning, you actually summoned a monster, which, one, you have to locate the monster, use coincidental-magic for it just to happen to show up. But, the hardest part would be, locating a monster or another supernatural-creature. <br /> <br /> Magic-Missile: Prime 2, Forces 3, Matter either 2 or 5, vulgar/vain magik. You need to create an arrow, that like, shoots out of your hand; you can do that with Forces 3 by itself, maybe? But it always has to hit, so you have to pump quintessence into it. It probably will do Agg. <br /> <br /> Any others you got?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"740"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-22T07:24:34-08:00","id":1531291,"title":"","content":"I know in later editions of D&amp;D the summoning spell doesn't create the animal, it summons it from elsewhere, and some versions summon celestial or otherworldly versions. Here are some variants for that:<br /> <br /> Summoning a housecat sized animal from elsewhere is Correspondence 2, with a success threshold based on the number of animals and how familiar the mage is with them or their location.<br /> <br /> Summoning larger creatures is Correspondence 4, but the success threshold is similar.<br /> <br /> Summoning otherworldly types involves calling a spirit and giving it a body. It takes Spirit 3 to call a spirit, or bring one across the gauntlet, and Spirit 4 to control it. Many spirits can step across on their own or at least create an appropriate body so for some summonings the mage can just use Spirit if he provides appropriate resources (a Fire elemental might need a bonfire nearby).<br /> <br /> Other spirits will need a body, in which case the spell is Spirit 3/[Appropriate Pattern Sphere]/Prime 2.<br /> <br /> Drawing out a creature that is already present, such as bringing forth a swarm of cockroaches in NYC, is Mind 2 or 3 depending on what level of influence you are using.<br /> <br /> Drawing out a creature that isn't present but has a reasonable chance of showing up is Entropy 2/Mind 2 or 3.<br /> <br /> Finding an appropriate nearby creature and drawing it to the scene is Correspondence 2/Mind 2 or 3 (though if it is Mind 3, the mage needs to have Corr at rank 3 to use it at range.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> For attack spells, it is possible to cast a spell that attacks directly rather than needing to be aimed. The spell can still fail if the Arete roll fails, but it won't miss if it goes off. Which way you adjudicate it has more to do with narrative than systems. A bolt of force that moves from the mage to the target implies the narrative of aiming, but it doesn't actually demand it.<br /> <br /> So a pure energy bolt that unerringly hits the target would be Forces 3/Prime 2 if the mage is summoning the energy. No Quintessence is needed, though if it is added the spell will be Aggravated. Fire based forces attacks are naturally aggravated, but force based ones are not.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Melf's Acid Arrow creates a projectile of acid that flies towards the target. That would require Matter 2/Forces 2/Prime 2. Matter 2/Prime 2 summons the acid and Forces 2 throws it at the target. This one would need to be aimed. That's mostly a narrative decision;. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}